# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "Every operand delimiter TiddlyWiki reads"

# filters.js switches on five opening delimiters after an operator name: `[` for a literal, `<`
# for a variable, `{` for a text reference, `(` for a multi-valued variable, and `/` for a regular
# expression. It builds all five; the last two it also warns about as deprecated.
#
# A form the grammar does not read falls into the operator name, so `prefix/Some/` read as one
# operator called `prefix/Some/` and the operand vanished into the thing that takes it. The
# operator name stops at every delimiter the parser switches on, or it swallows the next one.

! A regular-expression operand takes the family every highlighter keeps for one

{{{ [prefix/Some/] }}}
#    ^^^^^^ keyword.operator.operator.filter.tiddlywiki5
#           ^^^^ string.regexp.tiddlywiki5

! Its flags ride in round brackets after the closing slash

{{{ [prefix/Some/(gi)] }}}
#           ^^^^ string.regexp.tiddlywiki5
#                 ^^ keyword.other.flag.regexp.tiddlywiki5

! A multi-valued variable operand reads as the single-valued one beside it

{{{ [tag(multiValued)] }}}
#    ^^^ keyword.operator.operator.filter.tiddlywiki5
#        ^^^^^^^^^^^ variable.entity.filter.operand.tiddlywiki5
